==Abstract==
A SmartAccount has a stable receiving address and can transfer assets with built-in custody authorization. Optional contracts implement spending credentials, policy hooks, governance and recovery. This unnumbered proposal defines the core that calls them. It does not activate a network service.

==Motivation==
A wallet needs one operation encoding, replay domain and account identity regardless of which modules the owner installs. A module must not reinterpret a custody witness, a message signature or a successful preview as approval for an unrelated transfer.

==Specification==
The normative core rules are in the accompanying [[../SA-MODULE-PROTOCOL.md|SmartAccount protocol]], §§1–15, 17–18. Its [[smartaccount-native-profile-parameters.json|parameter table]] fixes numeric limits. The [[nep-smartaccount-module-interface.mediawiki|module interface]] defines discovery and callbacks; the [[nep-smartaccount-module-profiles.mediawiki|named profiles]] define what each standard module may authorize. These documents specify one wire format.

===Identity and execution===
An account identifier is scoped to a network and core. A wallet MUST use the derived account address when receiving assets. To execute, the caller supplies the account identifier separately and passes the operation prescribed by §4.1. It is a NeoVM Array of exactly 13 items, in order:
<pre>
[version, target, method, args, nonce, nonceSubkey, deadline, validatorId,
 credentialGeneration, authorityEpoch, configurationNonce, signature, hookProofs]
</pre>
A conforming core MUST reject any other field count. It MUST validate types, widths, lengths and canonical encoding before execution. For signing, replace only the signature with an empty ByteString, retain hook proofs, serialize the resulting array and bind it to the domain specified in §4.2–4.3. The submitted operation still requires a nonempty signature field in the selected route's format. A message signature uses §8 and does not authorize spending.

The core MUST check the nonce channel, credential generation, authority epoch, configuration nonce and applicable deadline. A validator authorizes; hooks constrain the authorized operation. Pre-checks run in installed order and post-checks in reverse over the same snapshot. Callback phases, code binding and failure handling follow §§7, 9–12. The core sets no per-call or aggregate GAS budget (§9.4, §11.1). A failed required callback cannot leave a partially committed operation. Transaction fees are accounted for separately.

===Authority and recovery===
Built-in custody permits basic transfers without installing a module. Once mandatory multisignature spending is enabled, custody alone MUST NOT spend, remove the policy, restore the built-in route, or change the recovery policy. Exiting that policy requires the preset delayed recovery process. The core reads no module policy body. It keeps the mandatory route installed, accepts module administration and an ordinary thaw only from the account's code-bound governance caller, and requires a usable final route before a configuration group commits. The standard governance and threshold modules own the confirmed policy, the delay and the confirmation window. A failing or unreadable validator MUST NOT fall back to built-in custody authorization. The named recovery profile specifies recipient consent, request maturity, cancellation and completion.

==Rationale==
The core retains the decisions needed to protect assets. The common interface lets a wallet inspect an optional module; a named profile tells the user what that module is allowed to do. An account receiving its first payment requires neither an installation nor a module discovery call.

==Backwards Compatibility==
This draft is unpublished. It specifies no alternate envelope and no fallback decoder.

==Test Cases==
Use the [[../../tests/fixtures/sa-module-protocol/golden-vectors/operation-envelope.json|operation vectors]] and [[../../tests/fixtures/sa-module-protocol/golden-vectors/nonce-channel.json|nonce vectors]] for encoding. Check that a submission with fewer or more fields is rejected, that changing a signed hook proof changes the digest, and that a stale credential generation cannot authorize a fresh operation. Module-specific requirements and their test cases are in the companion proposals.

==Implementation==
The [[../../contracts/native/SmartAccount|native implementation]], [[../../tests/fixtures/native-smartaccount.manifest.json|ABI fixture]] and [[../../contracts/native/profile.json|implementation status]] are distinct from the specification. Local tests or a manifest match do not establish public activation or universal compiler and VM correspondence.
